{
  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_002 (RNA, multiprofile, PCA-2000, avgpool) achieved 0.591 accuracy, a +0.106 gain over the single-profile baseline (config_001, 0.485), confirming that multiprofile aggregation is a major performance driver. However, we have only tested one aggregation strategy (avgpool) and do not yet know whether the aggregation mechanism itself matters or whether avgpool is simply 'good enough.' Question: config_003 changes only the aggregation from avgpool to transformer while holding modality (RNA), profile setting (multiprofile), and encoder (PCA-2000) fixed relative to config_002, giving a clean single-variable comparison that isolates the effect of aggregation strategy. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It is the cleanest possible 1-factor comparison in the current design space, avoiding confounds from encoder or modality changes. (2) The aggregation choice is a critical design axis that will directly affect all downstream multimodal configurations—knowing whether transformer aggregation adds value over simple averaging determines whether we should invest budget in transformer-based fusion later. (3) If transformer significantly outperforms avgpool, it reveals a high-value design lever; if it performs similarly or worse, it confirms avgpool is sufficient and redirects budget toward encoder or modality questions. (4) Under a limited budget, resolving this uncertainty early prevents committing multiple multimodal experiments to a suboptimal aggregation strategy."
}

